    "https://careers.uidaho.edu/jobs/associate-vice-president-of-principal-giving-moscow-idaho-united-states": {
      "title": "Associate Vice President of Principal Giving",
      "description": "Associate Vice President of Principal Giving - Moscow, Idaho, United States Skip to main content Applicant resources Benefits About Job search My Profile Kiosk mode Associate Vice President of Principal Giving 492454 Moscow, Idaho, United States Staff Opening on: Nov 2 2026 Add to favorites View favorites Appointment Information Working Title: Associate Vice President of Principal Giving, UI Foundation Division/College: 3913-Development Department: 604-FDN Development Location: Moscow Location Details: This position could work from Moscow or Boise FTE: 1 Advertising Pay Range: $160,000 annually, commensurate with experience Is this position contingent on work and/or funding: False FLSA Status: Exempt Type of Appointment: Full Fiscal Year Position Overview: The University of Idaho Foundation partners with alumni, friends, and philanthropic leaders to advance the university’s mission through private support. The Principal Gifts position will establish and develop a strategic principal gifts program to secure transformational philanthropic investments aligned with university priorities. This role will build the strategies, relationships, and cross-campus partnerships needed to identify, cultivate, and advance significant gift opportunities, creating a sustainable program that strengthens philanthropic support and drives lasting impact for the University of Idaho. This position is responsible for managing assigned principal gift prospects through strategic cultivation, face-to-face visits, proposal development, and the identification, cultivation, and solicitation of principal gifts, primarily of $5 million or more, from individuals, corporations, and foundations. The incumbent develops and executes strategies for complex, transformational gifts, aligning donor interests with institutional priorities and strategic initiatives. A key member of the foundation team, the incumbent collaborates with institutional leadership and development colleagues to identify and advance opportunities for transformational philanthropy. The position serves as a strategic resource on principal gifts, coordinates complex opportunities across units, and helps develop compelling funding strategies that support the university’s highest priorities. The incumbent will contribute to principal giving strategy and portfolio management while balancing independent initiative with a highly collaborative, team-oriented approach. The position may have supervisory responsibilities and will help advance the university’s most significant philanthropic opportunities and fundraising goals. Duties may include: Identify, cultivate and manage assigned prospects Collaborate with university leadership, development officers, academic departments and other stakeholders on fundraising campaigns and initiatives Develop and present principal gift level fundraising proposals or higher Develop and implement annual plans to raise money to resource the priorities of the university Build the overall principal giving program, including communications, stewardship, annual giving, planned giving, timelines and accountabilities Utilize data analytics and reporting tools to track and analyze giving metrics, donor trends and fundraising progress Supervision of employees Other duties as assigned Positions at this level typically manage a portfolio of donors rated primarily at $500,000 or more. They will typically raise a minimum of $10,000,0000 annually, in collaboration with other development officers. Required Experience: Eight years in fundraising or related work such as high-level sales, marketing, constituent-based outreach Five years as a supervisor OR five years recruiting, organizing and managing volunteers Required Education: Bachelor’s Degree Required Other: Ability to travel Ability to work nonstandard business hours , including weekends and holidays Must possess a valid driver’s license and be able to meet policy requirements for driving university-owned vehicles Additional Preferred: Education: Master’s Degree; JD Degree; CPA or CFP certification Experience in/with: Extensive major gift fundraising Development activities, preferably in higher education Securing major gifts and/or planned gifts Public relations or marketing Database computer systems and applications, preferably with customer relationship management systems Physical Requirements and Working Conditions: None Posting Information Open Until Filled: True Special Instructions to Applicants: To receive first consideration, please submit a letter of qualification and current resume no later than November 19, 2026. In your letter of qualifications ( https://www.uidaho.edu/human-resources/applicant-resources ) using details and examples, please address all of the required and preferred qualifications for this position and how your experience and skills align. If you would like to see the full job description, please contact Christy Day at cday@uidaho.edu. Visa Sponsorship: No Degree Requirement Timeline: Degree required at time of hire Back to job search Apply Now First Name (required) 831758c3 Last Name (required) 7a191a18 Email (required) 70c8cd5d Refer someone to this job Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Recommended Jobs Instructional Laboratory Coordinator Moscow Provost Coordinates the day-to-day operation of the Physics Educational Laboratories. Supports graduate teaching labs by guiding graduate teaching assistants (TAs) in effective instructional practices and techniques and ensures laboratory courses have adequate materials and content for instruction. Maintains the facilities, equipment and materials used for lecture demonstrations and laboratory experiments. Outside the academic year, duties could include undergraduate laboratory teaching during the summer session, laboratory exercise revision, preparation of course documents for upcoming academic year, inventory and equipment maintenance and other associated duties. Regular Faculty Moscow Provost The Physics Department at the University of Idaho is seeking to hire one tenure-track faculty member at the assistant professor level. We seek a faculty member who will establish strong research programs in experimental, theoretical, or computational physics that will complement or build on existing strengths within the department and the College of Science. General Technician I, II, or III Auxiliary Services Moscow Finance and Administration The Gen Tech I, II or III position works in Campus Services providing maintenance and building support to their assigned area. This role reports to Campus Services, the largest unit in Auxiliary Services, and works primarily in the residence halls and multi-use facilities (Idaho Student Union Building, ICCU Arena, PF1CU Kibbie Dome and others). This role is critical in providing routine and preventive maintenance and ensuring all buildings under Campus Services are maintained at the highest level for the students, staff, and community members who utilize our services. Background Check Statement: Applicants who are selected as final possible candidates must be able to pass a criminal background check EEO Statement: The University of Idaho is an equal employment opportunity employer, including veterans and individuals with disabilities. Sign up for job notifications Job Alert 4bdac1da Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories Development, Fundraising and Constituency Experience Development, Fundraising and Constituency Experience Employment Type Staff Staff Locations Moscow, Idaho, United States Moscow, Idaho, United States First Name (required) 0e45df72 Last Name (required) 1c87547c Email (required) 63e02586 Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Equal Opportunity Statement The University of Idaho is an equal employment opportunity employer, including veterans and individuals with disabilities. Request an accommodation University of Idaho is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in our services, programs, activities, education and employment. We encourage all qualified applicants to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Accommodation for job applicants is available during the application and/or hiring process. To request accommodation, please visit the Applicant Resources page. Facebook X Instagram LinkedIn Quick Links Human Resources Request an Accommodation Contact Us © 2026 University of Idaho Cookie Preferences Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all Please choose the location(s)"
