    "https://careers.uidaho.edu/jobs/research-faculty-fish-nutrition-moscow-idaho-united-states": {
      "title": "Research Faculty - Fish Nutrition",
      "description": "Research Faculty - Fish Nutrition - Moscow, Idaho, United States Skip to main content Applicant resources Benefits About Job search My Profile Kiosk mode Research Faculty - Fish Nutrition 492444 Moscow, Idaho, United States Faculty Opening on: Oct 28 2026 Add to favorites View favorites APPOINTMENT INFORMATION Division/College College of Agricultural & Life Sciences Department Department of Animal, Veterinary and Food Sciences Position Classification Research Faculty Rank Assistant Professor Tenure Status Tenure Track Location Moscow FTE 1.0 Advertising Pay Range Commensurate with Experience FLSA Status Exempt Type of Appointment Academic Year Anticipated Contract Start Date 08/17/2026 POSITION OVERVIEW Faculty Position Summary The University of Idaho seeks an exceptional candidate for an academic year tenure-track Assistant Professor position focused on fish nutrition and commercial aquaculture production. The successful candidate will have access to the Aquaculture Research Institute (ARI) facilities in Moscow and the Hagerman Fish Culture Experiment Station, as well as equipment at the Bozeman Fish Technology Center through a cooperative agreement with the USDA/ARS Trout Feeds Program. These outstanding facilities create unique opportunities to participate in regional, national, and international aquaculture research. This position serves Idaho’s aquaculture industry. Idaho is a leader in the national aquaculture industry, producing over 70% of the nation’s commercially grown rainbow trout and supporting production of sturgeon, tilapia, catfish, ornamental species, and conservation hatcheries. The faculty member will conduct and publish research in fish nutrition, secure external funding, mentor graduate students, and contribute to professional service and departmental activities in alignment with university and college expectations. Teaching responsibilities include 3–6 credits per year at the undergraduate and/or graduate level, primarily in monogastric nutrition. The position also involves advising students and supervising staff and postdoctoral fellows within the department and ARI. Teaching & Advising Effort 15% Scholarship & Creative Activity Effort 65% Outreach & Extension Effort 0% University Service & Leadership Effort 20% REQUIRED EDUCATION Ph.D. in Fish Nutrition, Animal Science, Aquaculture, Biology, or related to the domain of the position REQUIRED EXPERIENCE Experience competing for external funding Experience publishing research in refereed journals Experience delivering course instruction in a formal educational or training setting. Experience interacting with students or stakeholders through problem solving, communication, and professional engagement PREFERRED EXPERIENCE Experience teaching undergraduate and graduate courses Experience with verbal and written communication Experience in statistical analysis and interpretation of data Experience advising undergraduate students and mentoring graduate students Experience conducting research with commercial industry stakeholders and research sponsors PHYSICAL REQUIREMENTS AND WORKING CONDITIONS Must be able to obtain a valid driver’s license POSTING INFORMATION Open Until Filled Yes Contingency on Appointment No Special Instructions to Applicants Applications received by July 24, 2026 will receive first consideration. In addition, your application materials must include a CV, names & contact information for at least three (3) professional references, unofficial transcripts, statement of Research and a statement of Teaching. Visa Sponsorship Available for Appointment No Degree Requirement Timeline Listed degree qualification is required at time of hire. Back to job search Apply Now First Name (required) 921d6d9a Last Name (required) 6cfb13a2 Email (required) 33ae86cc Refer someone to this job Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Recommended Jobs Instructional Laboratory Coordinator Moscow Provost Coordinates the day-to-day operation of the Physics Educational Laboratories. Supports graduate teaching labs by guiding graduate teaching assistants (TAs) in effective instructional practices and techniques and ensures laboratory courses have adequate materials and content for instruction. Maintains the facilities, equipment and materials used for lecture demonstrations and laboratory experiments. Outside the academic year, duties could include undergraduate laboratory teaching during the summer session, laboratory exercise revision, preparation of course documents for upcoming academic year, inventory and equipment maintenance and other associated duties. Regular Faculty Moscow Provost The Physics Department at the University of Idaho is seeking to hire one tenure-track faculty member at the assistant professor level. We seek a faculty member who will establish strong research programs in experimental, theoretical, or computational physics that will complement or build on existing strengths within the department and the College of Science. General Technician I, II, or III Auxiliary Services Moscow Finance and Administration The Gen Tech I, II or III position works in Campus Services providing maintenance and building support to their assigned area. This role reports to Campus Services, the largest unit in Auxiliary Services, and works primarily in the residence halls and multi-use facilities (Idaho Student Union Building, ICCU Arena, PF1CU Kibbie Dome and others). This role is critical in providing routine and preventive maintenance and ensuring all buildings under Campus Services are maintained at the highest level for the students, staff, and community members who utilize our services. Background Check Statement: Applicants who are selected as final possible candidates must be able to pass a criminal background check EEO Statement: The University of Idaho is an equal employment opportunity employer, including veterans and individuals with disabilities. Sign up for job notifications Job Alert 11d88